+
+# Scheduled posts become public at read time
+
+## Context
+
+Authors want to write a post now and have it go live later. Until then the
+post must exist only for its author; afterwards it must be public with no
+button pressed and nothing running at that moment. Ops wants to read the
+schedule from a terminal.
+
+## Decision
+
+- `posts.published_at` is a nullable text column holding a UTC ISO 8601
+  timestamp, written by `toISOString()` like `created_at`. `NULL` or a time
+  that has passed means public; a later time means scheduled.
+- There is no "published" flag and no job that flips one. Visibility is
+  decided by every read, through `Post.visibleTo(viewerId)`: public posts,
+  plus the viewer's own scheduled ones. The posts list, search and every
+  route that takes a post id go through it. The home page passes `null`, so
+  it shows public posts only, whoever is looking.
+- A scheduled post answers **404** to everyone but its author, on the edit,
+  update and delete routes as well as on its page. `PostPolicy.view` states
+  the same rule, but a policy denial is a 403, which would confirm the post
+  exists.
+- Timestamps are compared as text, in SQL and in `Post.isScheduled()`. That
+  is only correct while every value has the one format, so `publishAt` from
+  a form must carry a zone (`Z` or an offset) and is normalized to UTC before
+  it is stored.
+- `publishAt` on `PUT /posts/:id` replaces the date: absent or `null` clears
+  it. The edit form therefore sends the current date back unless the author
+  changes it.
+- `bun run console posts:scheduled` prints `<id>\t<title>\t<published_at>`
+  per scheduled post, soonest first, and nothing else on stdout.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Going live cannot be missed or run twice, because nothing runs. It also
+  cannot trigger anything: notifying people when a post goes live would need
+  a scheduled job, which this does not provide.
+- A query on `Post` that does not start from `Post.visibleTo()` shows
+  scheduled posts to anyone. New read paths (a feed, an API, a sitemap) must
+  start there.
+- A row written by hand in another format (no milliseconds, a space instead
+  of `T`, a non-UTC offset) can compare wrongly against the current time when
+  the two fall on the same day. Write `published_at` as `toISOString()` does.
+- The clock that decides is the server's.
